Here are comprehensive reference notes on Spring AOP Pointcut Expressions, specifically breaking down the execution designator syntax, patterns, and behaviors.

Core Execution Pattern Syntax

The execution pointcut designator is the most frequently used aspect matching mechanism. It follows this strict template:
\(\text{execution}(\underbrace{\text{modifiers-pattern?}}_{\text{Optional}}\quad \underbrace{\text{ret-type-pattern}}_{\text{Required}}\quad \underbrace{\text{declaring-type-pattern?}}_{\text{Optional}}\quad \underbrace{\text{name-pattern(param-pattern)}}_{\text{Required}}\quad \underbrace{\text{throws-pattern?}}_{\text{Optional}})\)
• ? Denotes Optionals: Modifiers, Declaring Types, and Throws clauses can be omitted entirely.

1. Modifiers Pattern

Restricts method matching based on visibility scopes.
• public: Matches only public methods.
• protected: Matches only protected methods.
• private: Matches only private methods (Note: Spring AOP proxies typically do not intercept private methods, but AspectJ bytecode weaving does).
• Omitted: Omitting this field defaults to matching any modifier visibility.

2. Return-Type Pattern (ret-type-pattern)

Determines what data types the target method returns.
• * (Wildcard): Matches any return type (void, primitives, objects).
• Exact Type: Matches a specific primitive or class (e.g., void, java.lang.String, com.example.dto.UserDTO).
• + (Subclass Wildcard): Appending + matches the specific type and all its subclasses or implementations.
	• Example: com.example.dto.BaseDTO+ matches BaseDTO, UserDTO, OrderDTO.
• Package/Subpackage Modifiers:
	• .*: Matches any return type explicitly residing within that package level.
	• ..*: Matches any return type inside that package, its subpackages, or its deep sub-subpackages.

3. Declaring-Type Pattern

Defines the class or package boundary where the target methods exist. This is where package paths are resolved.
• com.example.demo.UserService: Matches only methods inside the UserService class.
• com.example.demo.*: Matches methods in any class directly inside the demo package.
• com.example.demo..*: Matches methods in any class inside demo, its subpackages, or its sub-subpackages.

4. Name Pattern (name-pattern)

Targets the literal name of the method using strings and wildcards.
• Exact Match: getUserById matches only that specific name.
• Wildcard (*): Matches any method name.
• Prefix Pattern (prefix*): Matches methods starting with a specific word (e.g., get* matches getName, getId).
• Suffix Pattern (*suffix): Matches methods ending with a specific word (e.g., *Save matches userSave, orderSave).

5. Parameter Pattern (param-pattern)

Matches methods based on their method signatures and argument arrays.
• (): Matches methods that take zero arguments.
• (..): Matches methods with any number of arguments (zero or more), of any data type.
• (*): Matches methods with exactly one argument of any type.
• (*, *): Matches methods with exactly two arguments of any type.
• (String): Matches methods with exactly one argument of type String. (Fully qualified paths like java.lang.String are preferred).
• (String, int): Matches methods taking exactly two arguments: a String followed by a primitive int.
• (String, ..): Matches methods where the first argument is a String, followed by any number (or zero) additional arguments of any type.

Putting It All Together: Cheat Sheet Examples


Pointcut Expression	What it Matches
execution(public * *(..))	Any public method, anywhere, with any return type/arguments.
execution(* set*(..))	Any method starting with "set" with any return type/arguments.
execution(* com.example.service.UserService.*(..))	Every method inside the UserService class.
execution(* com.example.service..*.*(..))	Every method in the service package and all its sub/sub-subpackages.
execution(com.example.dto.BaseDTO+ *(..))	Any method that returns a BaseDTO or any of its child subclasses.
execution(* *(java.lang.String, ..))	Any method anywhere whose first argument is a String.


Combining Pointcut Expressions

You can chain multiple pointcut patterns together using standard logical operators:
• && (AND): Both conditions must be met.
• || (OR): Either condition can be met.
• ! (NOT): Excludes matching signatures.

1. The @annotation Designator

Targets methods that are marked with a specific custom or framework-level annotation.
• Match a specific annotation: Matches any method annotated with @LogExecutionTime regardless of its package or return type.java
@Around("@annotation(com.example.aspect.LogExecutionTime)")
Use code with caution.
• Combine Execution and Annotation (&&): Matches only public methods inside the service package AND its subpackages that are marked with @Transactional.java
execution(public * com.example.service..*.*(..)) && @annotation(org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional)
Use code with caution.

2. The within Designator

Limits matching to join points within certain types (classes or packages). It acts like a strict filter on the location of the code.
• Match everything inside a package:java
within(com.example.controller..)
Use code with caution.
• Combine Execution and Within (&&): Matches any method returning a String AND guarantees the class resides strictly inside the web controller layer.java
execution(String *.*(..)) && within(com.example.web..*)
Use code with caution.

3. The @within and @target Designators

These filter based on annotations applied at the Class level rather than the method level.
• @within(AnnotationType): Matches any method inside a class that has the specified annotation.java
// Matches all methods inside classes annotated with @RestController
@Before("@within(org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController)")
Use code with caution.
• @target(AnnotationType): Matches if the runtime target object has the specified annotation.java
// Matches methods executed on runtime objects annotated with @Service
@Around("@target(org.springframework.stereotype.Service)")
Use code with caution.

4. Advanced Scenarios Using Exclusion (!)

Exclusion is critical for performance to avoid proxying unwanted methods (like boilerplate getters/setters or security configurations).
• Exclude specific packages: Match everything in the com.example project, EXCEPT classes in the config or security subpackages.java
within(com.example..*) && !within(com.example.config..) && !within(com.example.security..)
Use code with caution.
• Exclude specific method names: Match all methods in the service layer EXCEPT those starting with get or find (read-only methods).java
execution(* com.example.service..*.*(..)) && !execution(* com.example.service..*.get*(..)) && !execution(* com.example.service..*.find*(..))
Use code with caution.

5. Reusable @Pointcut Declarations

Instead of writing inline strings everywhere, you can define named pointcuts using empty method declarations. This makes code clean and maintainable.
java
@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {

    // 1. Define the pointcut for all services
    @Pointcut("within(com.example.service..*)")
    public void inServiceLayer() {}

    // 2. Define the pointcut for web controllers
    @Pointcut("execution(public * com.example.web..*.*(..))")
    public void publicWebMethods() {}

    // 3. Combine them in advice declarations
    @Around("inServiceLayer() || publicWebMethods()")
    public Object logPerformance(ProceedingJoinPoint joinPoint) throws Throwable {
        // Advice logic runs here
        return joinPoint.proceed();
    }
}
